dership) but concerns (show-off, not open to new ideas). Candidate C strengths (quick correct decisions, calm in crisis, understands complicated tech) but concerns (communication difficulty, egocentric, not willing to further education). Also crew cooperation positive per Sarah.\n\nWe need to make a focused contribution: maybe argue that Candidate C's strong crew cooperation and calmness are offset by egocentric attitude and low openness, making them less suitable than Candidate B who is conscientious and handles stress well despite some interpersonal issues. Or we could propose a provisional recommendation for Candidate B, citing strengths and acknowledging concerns.\n\nWe need to reference Sarah's point about crew cooperation positive for C. Then bring in our own facts: C's egocentric, not willing to further education, difficulty communicating ideas. That may affect crew cooperation long term. So maybe we shift to Candidate B as better overall.\n\nLet's craft: \"I appreciate Sarah's note on C's crew cooperation, but I am concerned about C's egocentric attitude and low openness, which could undermine long-term teamwork. In contrast, Candidate B is very conscientious, handles stress well, and assesses weather accurately\u2014key for long-haul flights\u2014though he has a weak memory for numbers and makes nasty remarks. The stress resilience and conscientiousness outweigh the interpersonal concerns, making B the leading candidate for now.\"\n\nVote for Candidate B.\n\nProceed."}
