s, solves problems, takes responsibility seriously, but arrogant, weak leadership, moody, loner, hot-tempered, know-it-all.\n\nWe have discussed all candidates. The current leading candidate is C per Emily and James. Sarah should now weigh in. She may either maintain C or shift. She should consider the new evidence: C's responsibility (100% reliable) and low adaptability. She may also consider A's strengths and concerns. She might argue that A's lack of evidence on reliability and stress resilience, plus concerns about criticism, make A less suitable. D's lack of crew cooperation and hot temper also problematic. B's lack of decision quality evidence and nasty remarks also problematic. So C remains best despite adaptability concerns. So Sarah likely votes for Candidate C.\n\nShe should reference prior discussion: James's note about B lacking decision quality, Emily's support for C, Sarah's own earlier points about A's concerns, James's note about D's lack of crew cooperation, etc.\n\nThus produce a concise argument: \"I still recommend Candidate C because...\". Provide evidence: 100% reliable, quick correct decisions, positive crew atmosphere, calm in crisis; concerns: egocentric, low openness, but still outweigh B's lack of decision quality and interpersonal issues, and A and D have bigger gaps.\n\nThus vote for Candidate C.\n\nNow produce output with PUBLIC_MESSAGE and METADATA_JSON."}
